    "Notes Updated": "9/15/2021",
    "Scouting Notes": "Fastball: Coming out of college sitting in the low 90's with his fastball, Ashby is now throwing mid upper 90's consistently with some serious sink. There have been some jaw dropping pitches in his time in the majors and it's generating a ton of ground balls. He mixes a rare 4FB in as well, and I'd like him to continue to do that. Think it could be a nice putaway pitch for him at times.\n\n Slider: The slider is looking like an absolutely elite pitch for Ashby. Since his debut, there has only been one hit off of the slider and it was an infield hit. It's generating a whiff rate of 44.7% and a xWOBA of 0.114. This is the type of pitch that can carry an entire repertoire. \n\n Changeup: The changeup is a pitch that Ashby developed in pro ball and it has come around to look like a very solid, major league pitch. Ashby is generating whiffs on 26.5% of the changeup's he's thrown in MLB, though he hasn't been able to master it as a \"\"put away\"\" pitch yet, having only struck out one batter on the change. \n\n Curveball: The curve has not been used a ton in MLB and the results aren't great, but I think it's a decent enough pitch, especially when it's the 3rd best secondary. Brandon Woodruff's curve wouldn't be considered a great pitch, but it does the job as his fourth offering. That should be the goal for Ashby as well.\n \n Command will be what ultimately decides whether Ashby can be a MLB starter. It comes and goes at the moment. Because of this Jekyll and Hyde situation with his command, there's definitely some risk that Ashby is a reliever long term. But even if that's the case, he's still probably a high leverage, multi inning relief weapon. Brewers fans know as well as anybody, how valuable that type of pitcher can be. The good news is, that he still has shown an ability to avoid walking batters as a major leaguer. It's more of an issue of commanding where in the zone his pitches will end up, than it is of him commanding his pitches into the strike zone in general.",
